Document Collection Manifest Generator generates duplicate import names for Korean file names
It seems that there is a problem with the Document Collection Manifest generator. When using a file name in Korean, the generator is unable to create an appropriate import name, so it creates an import name based on the first alphabet of the extension. This causes an issue by creating a duplication of the import name.
// NOTE This file is auto-generated by Contentlayer
import test23Md from './test23.md.json' assert { type: 'json' }
import mdx from './개발자 키리 - 대한민국 최애 개발자.mdx.json' assert { type: 'json' }
import mdx from './테스트용마크다운.mdx.json' assert { type: 'json' }
import 2____mMdx from './한글2____m.mdx.json' assert { type: 'json' }

export const allPosts = [helloWorldMd, test23Md, mdx, mdx, 2____mMdx]
Screen shot
My content directory

Auto generated 'document collection manifest'

OffsetDateTime -> PrimitiveDateTime
We can use assume_utc and others to convert a PrimitiveDateTime to an OffsetDateTime.
But there does not seem to be an easy way of converting back to a PrimitiveDateTime, that means: simply dropping the offset information.
Use case: Use PrimitiveDateTime for something like a database field, at which no offset information is required (UTC always implied). We still want to use "now" functions. As there is only OffsetDateTime::now_utc() and no PrimitiveDateTime::now_***(), a conversion only seems possible with
{
    let now = time::OffsetDateTime::now_utc();
    time::PrimitiveDateTime::new(now.date(), now.time())
}

I think there should either be a From/Into implementation or an appropriately named function for some or both types, which lets you do this using a single function call.

Node cannot find peer dependency of locally installed modules
Version
node --version v16.16.0
Platform
Microsoft Windows NT 10.0.20348.0 x64
Subsystem
No response
What steps will reproduce the bug?
I'm locally developing some modules.  I have both modules building correctly.
linql.core
linql.client
In my node app, when I import linql.client and attempt to use it, node throws the following:
//Uncaught Error Error [ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND]: Cannot find package 'linql.core' imported from Typescript\dist\linql.client\fesm2015\linql.client.mjs
When I try and use linql.client directly though, it has no issue finding the module.
I've tried the following with no luck:
"--experimental-modules",
"--experimental-specifier-resolution=node",
"--es-module-specifier-resolution=node"

Any help is much appreciated, as I've spent quite some time trying to figure this one out.
Example code
Clone this git repository: https://github.com/KrisSodroski/NodeImportExample
In the root of the project:
npm i
npm run setupcore
npm run setupclient
Navigate to examples/node
run the following:
npm i
npx tsc 
node ./dist/main.js
You will get this error:
//Uncaught Error Error [ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND]: Cannot find package 'linql.core' imported from ...\Typescript\dist\linql.client\fesm2015\linql.client.mjs
Now modify app.ts as so (linql.client is completely removed from the imports):
//import { DataModelClient } from "linql.client";
import { CoreModel } from "linql.core";

class LinqlNodeExample
{
    async Run()
    {

        //This doesn't work because it says it can't find linql.core
        //Uncaught Error Error [ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND]: Cannot find package 'linql.core' imported from C:\testModuleImport\Typescript\dist\linql.client\fesm2015\linql.client.mjs
        // const test = new DataModelClient();
        // console.log(test);
        //This does work, so obviously it can find linql.core
        const test2 = new CoreModel();
        console.log(test2);
    }

}

export async function Main(): Promise<any>
{

    const example = new LinqlNodeExample();
    await example.Run();

}
run the following:
npm i
npx tsc 
node ./dist/main.js
Notice the app has no issue running, and has no problem finding linql.core
How often does it reproduce? Is there a required condition?
Its always possible to reproduce when using "file:" packages that reference one another.
What is the expected behavior?
Node should be able to find the peer dependency that is locally installed and appears in node_modules.
What do you see instead?
Node is unable to resolve the peer dependency from a locally mapped module that is itself locally mapped.
Additional information
No response

/status returns error 500
WiinAppDriver v1.3 Release Candidate 1 (1.2.99)](https://github.com/microsoft/WinAppDriver/releases/tag/v1.2.99)
GET /status returns error 500:
{"status":13," value":{"error":"unknown error","message":"An unknown error occurred in the remote end while processing the command."}}
Some tools call /status as their first operation, and the error stops them from proceeding further. This is mentioned in several other bug reports (see for example #1728 or #1739).
@DHowett Any update on this?

