Interpreter handles opaque inconsistently
This program
use vstd::prelude::*;

verus!{

fn main() { }

pub proof fn bar() {
    assert(vstd::std_specs::bits::u64_leading_zeros(0) == 64) by (compute_only);
}

}

uses u64_leading_zeros, which is defined as public and open:



      verus/source/pervasive/std_specs/bits.rs


         Line 655
      in
      2b607f7







           pub open spec fn u64_leading_zeros(i: u64) -> int 





Oddly, when the interpreter tries to run, when it reaches the point of trying to apply u64_leading_zeros, it does a lookup for the function's body and the lookup fails, specifically we get None here:



      verus/source/vir/src/interpreter.rs


         Line 1365
      in
      2b607f7







           match ctx.fun_ssts.get(fun) { 





where fun_ssts is passed into the interpreter from the state object in the ast_to_sst conversion.
If I inline the definition in the same file, there's no trouble finding and using the definition.
use vstd::prelude::*;

verus!{

fn main() { }

#[verifier::opaque]
pub open spec fn u64_leading_zeros(i: u64) -> int
    decreases i
{
    if i == 0 {
        64
    } else {
        u64_leading_zeros(i / 2) - 1
    }
}

pub proof fn bar() {
    assert(u64_leading_zeros(0) == 64) by (compute_only);
}

}
}

Is this expected behavior?  Or is something going wrong in the collection of fun_ssts?

Varying reference function arguments
Consider the following code:
https://godbolt.org/z/1rrj4E467
struct S
{
    int member;
};

int expensive_function(S& varying_arg)
{
    return varying_arg.member;
}

void g()
{
    S s_array[10];        // Array of varying S
    int result;           // Varying result
    int i;

    for (i = 0; i < 10; i++)
    {
        if (s_array[i].member > 0)
        {
            break;
        }
    }

    result = expensive_function(s_array[i]); // Compile error
}

Can this be made working please?
Passing varyings as reference and thus accessing struct members through . instead of -> is a huge boon to make the code consistent with other technologies and works perfectly when just calling directly with local variables.
Also note that the focus here is not to process a huge array where soa might be of interest, but just to be able to call a function in such a flexible way.

`dx fmt` command inserts extra space before comma in tuple patterns within for loops
Problem
When using the dx fmt, an extra space is inserted before the comma in tuple patterns within a for loop.
Steps To Reproduce
Steps to reproduce the behavior:

Create a Rust file with a function that includes a for loop iterating over tuples, as shown in the example below.
Run the dx fmt command on the file.
Observe that the formatted code includes an extra space before the comma in the tuple pattern.

Expected behavior
