  distributed under the License is distributed on an "AS IS" BASIS,
  WITHOUT WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND, either express or implied.
  See the License for the specific language governing permissions and
  limitations under the License.
 */
package org.apache.commons.cli;

/**
 * The class BasicParser provides a very simple implementation of the {@link Parser#flatten(Options,String[],boolean)
 * flatten} method.
 *
 * @deprecated since 1.3, use the {@link DefaultParser} instead
 */
@Deprecated
public class BasicParser extends Parser {

    /**
     * <p>
     * A simple implementation of {@link Parser}'s abstract {@link Parser#flatten(Options, String[], boolean) flatten}
     * method.
     * </p>
     *
     * <p>
     * <b>Note:</b> {@code options} and {@code stopAtNonOption} are not used in this {@code flatten} method.
     * </p>
     *
     * @param options The command line {@link Options}
     * @param arguments The command line arguments to be parsed
     * @param stopAtNonOption Specifies whether to stop flattening when an non option is found.
     * @return The {@code arguments} String array.
     */
    @Override
    protected String[] flatten(@SuppressWarnings("unused") final Options options, final String[] arguments, @SuppressWarnings("unused") final boolean stopAtNonOption) {
        // just echo the arguments
        return arguments;
    }
}

Processing the ClassOrInterfaceDeclaration defined within it (BasicParser) and extracting the method within it as a MethodDeclaration (the method flatten), calling the api getDeclarationAsString as in the following snippet of code:
cu.findAll(ClassOrInterfaceDeclaration.class).forEach(classDecl -> {
    String className = classDecl.getFullyQualifiedName().get().toString();
    for (MethodDeclaration method : classDecl.getMethods()) {
        ObjectNode methodJson = mapper.createObjectNode();
        methodJson.put("projectName", projectName);
        methodJson.put("className", className);
        methodJson.put("methodSignature", method.getDeclarationAsString(false, false, true));
        methodJson.put("methodSourceCode", method.toString());
        methodsArray.add(methodJson);
    }
});

Both the CompilationUnit, ClassOrInterfaceDeclaration, and MethodDeclaration lose the comments, resulting in the following content:
package org.apache.commons.cli;

@Deprecated
public class BasicParser extends Parser {

    @Override
    protected String[] flatten(@SuppressWarnings("unused") final Options options, final String[] arguments, @SuppressWarnings("unused") final boolean stopAtNonOption) {
        return arguments;
    }
}

Therefore, the following  method.toString() returns:
@Override
protected String[] flatten(@SuppressWarnings("unused") final Options options, final String[] arguments, @SuppressWarnings("unused") final boolean stopAtNonOption) {
    return arguments;
}

instead of:
/**
 * <p>
 * A simple implementation of {@link Parser}'s abstract {@link Parser#flatten(Options, String[], boolean) flatten}
 * method.
 * </p>
 *
 * <p>
 * <b>Note:</b> {@code options} and {@code stopAtNonOption} are not used in this {@code flatten} method.
 * </p>
 *
 * @param options The command line {@link Options}
 * @param arguments The command line arguments to be parsed
 * @param stopAtNonOption Specifies whether to stop flattening when an non option is found.
 * @return The {@code arguments} String array.
 */
@Override
protected String[] flatten(@SuppressWarnings("unused") final Options options, final String[] arguments, @SuppressWarnings("unused") final boolean stopAtNonOption) {
    // just echo the arguments
    return arguments;
}

And the whole CompilationUnit changes to
package org.apache.commons.cli;

@Deprecated
public class BasicParser extends Parser {

    @Override
    protected String[] flatten(@SuppressWarnings("unused") final Options options, final String[] arguments, @SuppressWarnings("unused") final boolean stopAtNonOption) {
        return arguments;
    }
}

Although is possible to overcome the problem with a method.clone().getDeclarationAsString(false, false, true), I suppose it is not a correct behavior and the api getDeclarationAsString should not have this side effect.
See the attached screen recording to analyze the unexpected behavior.





    Screen.Recording.mp4

`CommandModelParameterResolver` | Parameter resolver for entity state
Feature Description
I want to be able to resolve state needed in the command handler using annotation.
If state is loaded from async repository, wait for the completable future.
Something like:
@CommandHandler
   ??? handle(
            RecruitCreature command,
            @CommandModel State state
    ) {
        return decide(command, state);
    }

class State {
   String prop1; 

  @EventSourcingHandler
    void evolve(SomethingHappened event) {
        this.prop1 = event.prop1;
    }

}
Current Behaviour
With the current state of AF5 implementation if someone wants to execute some command against an entity, needs to load the entity from the repository invoking it directly, and dealing with CompletableFuture API (like blow), it's not "developer friendly" approach.
private final AsyncRepository<RecruitCreature.TargetId, State> stateRepository;
private final EventGateway eventGateway;

@CommandHandler
??? handle(
       RecruitCreature command,
       ProcessingContext processingContext
) {
   return stateRepository.load(command.targetId(), processingContext)
           .thenApply(ManagedEntity::entity)
           .thenApply(state -> decide(command, state))
           .thenAccept(eventGateway::publish);
}
Wanted Behaviour
There is an CommandModelParameterResolver
Possible Workarounds
Work with the Repository directly in the command handler.

Address calculations with int vs uint
Consider this:
https://godbolt.org/z/sebW8W5xP
varying int fetch_with_uint(uniform int* data, varying uint offset_uint) {
    return data[offset_uint];
}

varying int fetch_with_sint(uniform int* data, varying int offset_sint) {
    return data[offset_sint];
}

The resulting assembly is in both cases the same - in both cases the result of the address calculation seems to be that the offset overflows at 2^31.
This is quite different to regular C where the uint address calculation does not overflow until 2^32.
This is on purpose or can this be fixed?
Regards,
Lars

